#!/bin/sh
# fppd reads this script's stdout as a comma-separated list of the plugin
# types it provides, and a plugin with no `callbacks` file at all is a
# successful no-op that loads nothing: FPP 10.0's
# PluginManager::loadPlugin logs "has no callbacks; nothing for fppd to
# load" and returns true. So this file, not pluginInfo.json, is what makes
# the resident C++ component loadable at all.
#
# It prints exactly "c++" and nothing else, and that exclusivity is
# load-bearing rather than tidiness. ScriptFPPPlugin's constructor
# consumes "media", "playlist", and "lifecycle" into script callbacks, and
# PluginManager::loadUserPlugin then takes the SCRIPT path whenever
# hasCallback() is true and never reaches the dlopen branch. Declaring any
# of those three here would silently prevent the shared library from ever
# being loaded, while still looking like a working plugin. The resident
# component observes the playlist natively from inside fppd once loaded;
# it does not want a forked script callback.
#
# No path is given after "c++", so fppd uses its own default of
# lib<plugin-directory-name>.so, which is libfpp-showmesh.so here. The
# installer is what decides which version adapter earns that name: the
# built objects are libshowmesh-fpp9.so and libshowmesh-fpp10.so, and
# install-time FPP-major detection places the correct one at the single
# stable name this line resolves to. Selecting the adapter here instead
# would duplicate version detection the installer already does, in a
# script whose entire output budget is a 128-byte pipe read.
#
# fppd forks this script and reads stdout; it is not sourced. Keep the
# output to the single token and write nothing else to stdout.
printf 'c++'
